<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vortiporius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. mid-6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Gwrthefyr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vortiporius was a 6th-century king of Dyfed in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gildas">Gildas</a> called tyrant of the Demetae and condemned in On the Ruin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. A memorial stone from Castell Dwyran in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthenshire">Carmarthenshire</a>, inscribed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and ogham to Voteporigis the Protector, is often taken to be his. Welsh genealogies name him Gwrthefyr.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vortiporius.jpg" alt="Vortiporius" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LinguisticDemographer at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LDDyfedCantrefi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vortiporius, often shortened to Vortipor, ruled Dyfed, an area roughly corresponding to modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pembrokeshire">Pembrokeshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthenshire">Carmarthenshire</a>, in the early to mid-6th century. Records of the period are scant and almost nothing is known of him or his kingdom. The 8th-century Irish work The Expulsion of the Déisi calls him Gartbuir and traces his descent from Eochaid Allmuir, said to have led a sept of the Déisi in their settlement of Dyfed around 270. Harley MS 5389, written about 1100, gives a nearly identical pedigree with the name Guortepir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The only contemporary information comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gildas">Gildas</a>'s De Excidio et Conquestu Britanniae, written around 540. Gildas condemned five British kings by likening them to the beasts of the Book of Revelation, and he called Vortiporius the spotted leopard and the tyrant of the Demetians. He wrote that the king was grey with age, that his wife had died and that he had at least one daughter, and he called him the bad son of a good father.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gildas">Gildas</a> also attacked the daughter as shameless and implied that the king had a sexual relationship with her. Why Gildas turned against these kings is unknown; he passed over the rulers of Rheged, Gododdin, Elmet and Powys. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a>'s 12th-century Historia Regum Britanniae, Vortiporius succeeds Aurelius Conanus, defeats a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> fleet brought over from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and rules in peace for four years before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maelgwn-gwynedd">Maelgwn Gwynedd</a>, an account that comes from Geoffrey alone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>A memorial stone found in 1895 near the church of Castell Dwyran in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthenshire">Carmarthenshire</a>, carved with a Christian cross, reads Memoria Voteporigis Protictoris in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Votecorigas in ogham. The scholar Rhys at once took Voteporix and Vortiporius to be the same man, but Patrick Sims-Williams has argued that the missing r of the first syllable is significant, that the stone commemorates someone else, and that dating it to Vortiporius's time may not be valid. The ogham shows that Primitive Irish was still in use in this part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vortiporius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 6th century Gildas denounced its king <b>Vortiporius</b> as tyrant of the Demetae, and a memorial stone at Castell Dwyran bearing his name in Latin and ogham may be his.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dyfed">Kingdom of Dyfed</a></li></ul></section>
